---
title: On Prison Abolition
authors: [Matt Miller]
broadcast: 3
order: 3
start: "10:26"
---

In 1991, New York City held more than 21,000 people in its jails on any given day. By the spring of 2020, as bail reform took hold and the city rushed to release people amid the pandemic, that number fell under 4,000, the lowest it had been since 1946. No new jail opened and no capacity was added; the drop, one of the largest any American city has recorded, came almost entirely through policy rather than construction.

That fact sits underneath the whole abolitionist argument, even if abolitionists rarely lead with it. Angela Davis’s Are Prisons Obsolete? asks a more basic question first: why does prison feel like the only possible response to harm, when it’s a historically recent one? She traces a direct line from slavery through convict leasing, the practice of arresting Black Americans for invented offenses and renting their labor back out after emancipation, into the modern prison system. The prison system did not just echo slavery symbolically; it took over its economic function directly, and race has shaped who fills American jails and prisons ever since.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/matt-08.jpg" alt="Historical photograph of a convict leasing chain gang in striped uniforms standing along a railroad cut" ancho="completo" /%}

From there the argument turns from history to capacity. New York cut its jail population by roughly 80 percent through policy alone, which suggests the ceiling on incarceration is political rather than structural. Abolition, in this sense, does not call for opening every door at once. Grants to cultural and community institutions, a fraction of what jail capacity costs, do not answer that argument either; they read as a gesture alongside continued spending on capacity, not a redirection of it. The money, land, and will currently spent on capacity could instead fund housing, healthcare, income, and education, the conditions that make jail less necessary. New York’s own 2020 low shows that scale is achievable; the population has since climbed back over 6,000, evidence the city did not choose to hold that line.

In Chinatown, the argument is not abstract. No New Jails NYC formed in 2018 to argue that “modern” and “humane” jails still expand the same system they claim to fix, using a slogan blunt enough to survive a city council hearing: if they build it, they will fill it. That claim has a contractual basis: a 2015 review found that roughly two-thirds of private prison contracts include lockup quotas, clauses guaranteeing the operator a minimum occupancy rate, with the state required to pay for empty beds if the population falls below it. Groups working the same blocks the new jail is rising on, the Chinatown Art Brigade and the W.O.W. Project, have organized against it since the plan was announced, treating it as a continuation of Rikers rather than an improvement on it.

